You can now use[span queries](https://mastra.ai/reference/client-js/observability#querying-spans) to filter and group individual spans across all of your traces. Mastra platform and Studio support span filtering and grouping by time range, type, name, status, error, duration, and more. Your coding agent can run the same queries, then fix what it finds.


[Trace queries](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-trace-queries) can filter on details deep within a trace, like a failed tool call, but will return the whole trace, making problematic calls harder to spot. Span queries are more granular and search within each trace to return just the matching spans.


Your browser does not support the video tag.


Span queries can be used to group or aggregate across matching spans to produce measures like counts, error rates, and latency. Use them to spot which tool is failing or slow, or to track tool latency over time, then use span queries with the same filters to inspect the problematic calls. Each query can combine up to 100 conditions, using fields like` spanType` ,` entityName` ,` status` ,` durationMs` , and` parentEntityName` . Span queries return up to 1,000 spans per page, and span groups can group by up to 2 dimensions over a` timeRange` of up to 365 days.


Span queries work on Mastra platform and on self-hosted Mastra projects where` storage.observability` uses DuckDB, PostgreSQL, or ClickHouse. If your trace data lives outside of Mastra, you can bring your existing history over with[trace imports](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-trace-imports-for-mastra-platform) .


## Get started


Span queries need a platform access token and your project ID, sent with each request. Use the correct Mastra observability[base URL](https://mastra.ai/docs/mastra-platform/api) for your project's region:


- **US** :` https://observability.mastra.ai`
- **EU** :` https://observability.eu.mastra.ai`


There are four ways to run span queries: Mastra CLI, Mastra platform and Studio, Mastra Client SDK, and observability endpoints. Each code example below groups tool calls from the last 7 days by tool, then finds the tool calls that ended in an error.


### Mastra CLI


Install or upgrade the Mastra CLI globally:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   -g   mastra@latest
```


note


Requires` mastra@1.36.0` or later, added in[PR #28036](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/28036) .


Run the[mastra api span aggregate](https://mastra.ai/reference/cli/mastra#mastra-api-span-aggregate) command to see call count, error rate, and p95 latency for every tool. The CLI reads` MASTRA_PLATFORM_ACCESS_TOKEN` and` MASTRA_PROJECT_ID` from your environment:


Terminal


```text
mastra   api   --url   https://observability.mastra.ai   span   aggregate   '  {
"timeRange": { "from": "2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z", "to": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z" },
"where": { "op": "eq", "left": { "path": "spanType" }, "right": { "literal": "tool_call" } },
"groupBy": ["entityName"],
"measures": ["count", "errorRate", "duration.p95"],
"orderBy": { "field": "errorRate", "direction": "desc" }
}  '
```


Run the[mastra api span query](https://mastra.ai/reference/cli/mastra#mastra-api-span-query) command to find tool calls that ended in an error:


Terminal


```text
mastra   api   --url   https://observability.mastra.ai   span   query   '  {
"where": {
"op": "and",
"args": [
{ "op": "eq", "left": { "path": "spanType" }, "right": { "literal": "tool_call" } },
{ "op": "eq", "left": { "path": "status" }, "right": { "literal": "error" } }
]
}
}  '
```


### Mastra platform and Studio


On Mastra platform and Studio, the **Spans** tab lets you filter by time range, span type, span name, status, error, duration, and more.


### Mastra Client SDK


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/client-js@latest
```


note


Requires` @mastra/client-js@1.57.0` or later, added in[PR #25920](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/25920) and[PR #27827](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/27827) .


src/client.ts


```text
import   {   MastraClient   }   from   "  @mastra/client-js  "  ;


export   const   mastraClient   =   new   MastraClient  ({
baseUrl:   "  https://observability.mastra.ai  "  ,
headers: {
Authorization:   `  Bearer   ${  process.env.MASTRA_PLATFORM_ACCESS_TOKEN  }`  ,
"  X-Mastra-Project-Id  "  : process.env.MASTRA_PROJECT_ID  !
}
});
```


Use[aggregateSpans()](https://mastra.ai/reference/client-js/observability#aggregate-spans) to see call count, error rate, and p95 latency for every tool, with the worst offenders first:


src/aggregate-spans.ts


```text
import   {   mastraClient   }   from   "  ./client  "  ;


const   {   rows   }   =   await   mastraClient.  aggregateSpans  ({
timeRange: {
from:   "  2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z  "  ,
to:   "  2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z  "
},
where: {
op:   "  eq  "  ,
left: { path:   "  spanType  "   },
right: { literal:   "  tool_call  "   }
},
groupBy: [  "  entityName  "  ],
measures: [  "  count  "  ,   "  errorRate  "  ,   "  duration.p95  "  ],
orderBy: { field:   "  errorRate  "  , direction:   "  desc  "   }
});
```


Add an` interval` to track tool latency over time:


src/tool-latency.ts


```text
import   {   mastraClient   }   from   "  ./client  "  ;


const   {   rows   }   =   await   mastraClient.  aggregateSpans  ({
timeRange: {
from:   "  2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z  "  ,
to:   "  2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z  "
},
where: {
op:   "  eq  "  ,
left: { path:   "  spanType  "   },
right: { literal:   "  tool_call  "   }
},
groupBy: [  "  entityName  "  ],
interval:   "  1d  "  ,
measures: [  "  duration.p50  "  ,   "  duration.p95  "  ,   "  duration.p99  "  ]
});
```


Use[querySpans()](https://mastra.ai/reference/client-js/observability#querying-spans) to find tool calls that ended in an error:


src/spans.ts


```text
import   {   mastraClient   }   from   "  ./client  "  ;


const   {   spans   }   =   await   mastraClient.  querySpans  ({
timeRange: {
from:   "  2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z  "  ,
to:   "  2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z  "
},
where: {
op:   "  and  "  ,
args: [
{ op:   "  eq  "  , left: { path:   "  spanType  "   }, right: { literal:   "  tool_call  "   } },
{ op:   "  eq  "  , left: { path:   "  status  "   }, right: { literal:   "  error  "   } }
]
}
});
```


